6: the court at Xu governs only Cao Cao's heartland.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/End_of_the_Han_dynasty","c":1},{"y":204,"t":"loss","x":"204: the Gongsun hold Lelang, Daifang and Xuantu.","cp":"gongsun-liaodong","cpn":"Gongsun Liaodong","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four_Commanderies_of_Han","c":2},{"y":207,"t":"gain","x":"207: the north comes under the court.","cp":"yuan-shao","cpn":"Yuan Shao","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/End_of_the_Han_dynasty","c":2},{"y":211,"t":"gain","x":"211: Liang and Yong come under the court.","cp":"han-sui","cpn":"Han Sui","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/End_of_the_Han_dynasty","c":2},{"y":220,"t":"vassal_end","x":"220: the Southern Xiongnu tie runs to the end of the dynasty.","m":12,"cp":"southern-xiongnu","cpn":"Southern Xiongnu","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Han_dynasty","c":1},{"y":220,"t":"dissolve","x":"220: the Han dynasty ends.","m":12,"cp":"cao-wei","cpn":"Cao Wei","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Han_emperors","c":3}],"wars":[{"name":"Han-Xiongnu War","from":-133,"to":91,"outcome":"Infobox: 'Han victory - Destruction of the Xiongnu'; territorial changes 'Annexation of the Ordos Plateau and Hexi Corridor by the Han' and 'Establishment of Han suzerainty in the Western Regions'.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Han-Xiongnu_War"},{"name":"Han campaigns for the Western Regions","from":-108,"to":-60,"outcome":"Zhao Ponu took Loulan and Turfan in 108; the Turfan Depression came under 'absolute control' in 67; the Protectorate of the Western Regions was founded in 60.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Han_dynasty"},{"name":"Minyue's invasion of Nanyue, with the Han relief","from":-135,"to":-135,"outcome":"'In 135 BC, the king of neighboring Minyue launched an attack on the towns along the two kingdoms' borders'; Zhao Mo appealed to the Han, which sent Wang Hui and Han Anguo with an army to attack Minyue from two directions.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nanyue"},{"name":"Han conquest of Nanyue","from":-112,"to":-111,"outcome":"'By the year's end, the army had destroyed Nanyue and established Han rule'; the territory 'was divided into the seven commanderies of Nanhai, Cangwu, Yulin, Hepu, Jiaozhi, Jiuzhen, and Rinan.'","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nanyue"},{"name":"Gojoseon-Han War","from":-109,"to":-108,"outcome":"'the fall of Gojoseon', with the Four Commanderies set up 'in the northern half of the Korean Peninsula'; in April 108 BCE the last officials surrendered.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gojoseon-Han_War"},{"name":"Han campaigns against Dayuan","from":-104,"to":-101,"outcome":"'After a campaign that lasted four years, Li Guangli conquered Ferghana in 101 BC'; Dayuan came 'into submission'.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Han-Xiongnu_War"},{"name":"Guangwu's reunification","from":25,"to":36,"outcome":"'From 26 until 36 AD, Emperor Guangwu had to wage war against other regional warlords' (Han page); 'By AD 30, all of eastern China was under Emperor Guangwu's rule'; Chengdu surrendered in the winter of 36; 'By 37 AD, the Han dynasty was fully restored' (Xin…","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emperor_Guangwu_of_Han"},{"name":"Trưng rebellion","from":40,"to":43,"outcome":"'In March of 40 AD, Trưng Trắc and her younger sister Trưng Nhị led the Lạc Việt to rise up in rebellion'; Ma Yuan beheaded them in the first month of 43.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trưng_sisters"},{"name":"Qiang rebellion","from":107,"to":118,"outcome":"'widespread Qiang rebellion that lasted from 107 to 118 AD'; the protectorate in the Western Regions was abandoned on 29 July 107.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Han_dynasty"},{"name":"Yellow Turban Rebellion","from":184,"to":205,"outcome":"'Zhang Jue's massive rebellion across eight provinces was annihilated by Han forces within a year' (Han page); the End of the Han page gives the span '(184-205)'.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/End_of_the_Han_dynasty"},{"name":"Wars of the warlords","from":190,"to":220,"outcome":"The coalition against Dong Zhuo (spring 190); Cao Cao at Xu (196); Guandu (200); the north under Cao Cao (207); Red Cliffs (208); Yi Province to Liu Bei (215); Hanzhong (219); the abdication (winter 220).","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/End_of_the_Han_dynasty"}],"certainty":[{"from":-202,"to":220,"level":"soft","note":"The Han page gives infobox areas (6.0 M km2 in 50 BC, 6.5 M in 100 AD) but no boundaries; the frontiers with the steppe, the Tarim, the south-west and Korea are approximate, and the kingdoms of the early years are nominal Han."}],"sources":[{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Han_dynasty","title":"Han dynasty"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Han-Xiongnu_War","title":"Han-Xiongnu War"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nanyue","title":"Nanyue"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhao_Tuo","title":"Zhao Tuo"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protectorate_of_the_Western_Regions","title":"Protectorate of the Western Regions"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gojoseon-Han_War","title":"Gojoseon-Han War"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four_Commanderies_of_Han","title":"Four Commanderies of Han"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trưng_sisters","title":"Trưng sisters"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/End_of_the_Han_dynasty","title":"End of the Han dynasty"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emperor_Guangwu_of_Han","title":"Emperor Guangwu of Han"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xin_dynasty","title":"Xin dynasty"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wang_Mang","title":"Wang Mang"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Han_emperors","title":"List of Han emperors"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lelang_Commandery","title":"Lelang Commandery"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daemusin_of_Goguryeo","title":"Daemusin of Goguryeo"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L%C3%A2m_%E1%BA%A4p","title":"Lâm Ấp"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emperor_Gengshi_